00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in our series school matters we're  looking at the growing  teacher shortage in districts nationwide  the potential crisis is expected to  stretch into the fall  a recent recent survey looking at more  than a thousand districts found a record  two  in three reported having a teacher  shortage and  few were immune from it in cities 75  percent of districts reported a shortage  compared with 65 percent in rural areas  and 60 percent in the suburbs  meg oliver looks at what's behind the  problem and how it might be  fixed  these days scott sherburne's life feels  more free than free falling  but that wasn't always the case  especially this last year  how hard was it to say goodbye to your  students it was extremely difficult  to say goodbye to my students the  55-year-old spent 29 years teaching  science in livonia michigan  but after contracting covit in november  and juggling increased demands at work  the pandemic pushed him to retire early  in january  the middle of the school year did you  ever feel like you were letting your  students down retiring midway through  the year  yes very much so it was an extremely  difficult decision  if the pandemic hadn't happened would  you still have retired early  definitely not michigan has seen a 40  percent increase in the number of  teacher  retirements compared to last year  nationwide it's a similar story  nearly every state has reported  shortages and many districts are having  trouble  finding teachers to fill vacancies some  now  offering signing bonuses to help recruit  them have you ever had this many  teachers retire in one year  no and will you be able to fill these  positions we will work very hard to fill  these positions  i'm worried you're worried i'm worried  karen bingard is the principal at  hillsborough high school in new jersey  since last july 56 staff members in the  district have retired  or are planning to by the end of this  month 38 of them  are teachers that currently leaves  around two dozen positions  open at the high school alone why are we  seeing this national teacher shortage  i think it's been coming actually for  several years  there are fewer candidates going into  education to begin with  i think some of that stems from the fact  that over time  just there's been less interest there's  been a little bit of public pushback  on the respect with which teachers are  treated  pay has also been a deterrent from many  teachers who feel they aren't  compensated properly for the demands of  the job  and in the pandemic we all saw the  challenges teachers had to face with  remote learning  if it wasn't for the pandemic do you  think some of these teachers who are  retiring may have just  held on a little longer think the  pandemic was certainly  perhaps the last straw so how do you  turn the teacher shortage around  what needs to happen some of it is just  the messaging that exists out in the  world  education is a phenomenal career  it is it sparks joy  [Music]  for sherburne the joy of working with  students was the hardest to leave behind  but for now he says he doesn't regret  retiring early  all in on education i'm all in on  teaching  uh and yes i felt i felt a good amount  of guilt  and trepidation as i made that decision  but it for for  myself and my family it was what we felt  we had to do  at that time sherburne now co-owns a  real estate business  in an effort to recruit certified  teachers in new jersey there is a bill  working its way through the senate that  would allow retired teachers to come  back for up to two years receiving a  salary  and their pension to help with the  shortage principal bingert applauded  that move  yeah it's been such a tough year for  well for everybody but particularly for  teachers too and i know so many of them  felt  incredibly stressed including my wife  who's a teacher as i've just said but  it's uh you know it's it's a real  dilemma now because there was a teacher  shortage before this  it's only gotten worse meg thank you

